#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512976} :activity/id #uuid "5d2d2b52-5ff3-5595-845a-94f1c9e2f4a2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049288 :topic/id #uuid "ea0735e9-7712-5a1c-a626-25cab33f54cb" :topic/math-academy-id 6386} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512976 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512900 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0da3558a-014b-530b-8b42-aea977dc4e14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512125} {:answer/id #uuid "62559ed5-80cf-5704-bf90-5935af3f40e7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512332} {:answer/id #uuid "9009e0f7-5d50-5aa5-a503-ea03f3e99675" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512439} {:answer/id #uuid "914f8df8-af61-5f9e-a17f-75583f136c10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512444}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512439} :answer-field/id #uuid "58617cc3-c088-55aa-805f-3f4f74b88173" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512774} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20e270f2-1405-5a3a-bdf2-62a39dd31496" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}{\\pi }^{2}}{\\pi +{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512168} {:answer/id #uuid "283b9537-1996-5ae6-a737-f53e608ac594" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}(1-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2})}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512189} {:answer/id #uuid "e84259ba-5de4-5201-9e89-fe85cb2ea4c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi (1+{n}^{2}\\pi )}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512654} {:answer/id #uuid "e9dd1e71-1b42-5658-b30c-6f8df61b1ce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+1)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512661}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512661} :answer-field/id #uuid "b53af9ba-07ea-5f68-ae7e-da94231dbd26" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512822}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "7658d4f4-3b1c-50cc-bb09-9642192fb8ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-342372" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $2$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that all the sine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}n\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-{a}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)-(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x))=1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n-\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}(-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}-1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x)=1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=1 \\\\ {a}_{n}(-{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}-1)=\\frac{(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-1 \\\\ {a}_{n}=\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+1)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+1)}\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "38154cfd-c70d-53d6-a6c1-72899f8c40bb" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513019}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512998 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317451 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0775efd4-349e-5347-a0ab-209c8617865e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{6x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315155} {:answer/id #uuid "3c6860ca-41e8-5f7c-9922-b06de92ebf64" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315465} {:answer/id #uuid "a683ea58-3eb4-513d-ae1d-5c5ee8f548e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{12x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316056} {:answer/id #uuid "f37afc9b-a1f3-5052-8c94-74b4bd652d0b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316477}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315465} :answer-field/id #uuid "69639b9f-3bb7-58ec-a83d-c19f4137e2fc" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316800} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c8e5254-d482-5016-83a5-2d062da9f632" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315193} {:answer/id #uuid "464643b7-419c-5207-ac7e-9b750b301b15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315524} {:answer/id #uuid "85cff5d8-8660-5f09-837d-b9a7c81dfdb3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315864} {:answer/id #uuid "f2f486c4-6a97-5a85-bb1d-c46b9ac5c8b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316471}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186315864} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec2fdc4d-025d-5256-ba3f-588326c84d79" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317105}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "a67d7600-1f79-59aa-9051-5cda440b9948" :question/math-academy-id "q-343901" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x),$ where the $4$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼-2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{6}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $4$-periodic function $(L=2)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}))+x(-\\frac{\\pi }{2}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\frac{\\pi }{2}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}y=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}):$\n\n$$\n[-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{4}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\pi A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})+\\pi B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2})=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\pi A=0 \\\\ \\pi B=6\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=0 \\\\ B=\\frac{6}{\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\frac{6x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{2}).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7402cf38-a638-59a3-b9f9-c0865bae0160"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513000 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368351 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6f942acd-fc3f-553e-9236-b60c2312cab0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{3x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368353} {:answer/id #uuid "ee5c562e-9d36-5f0d-8f3c-9b02f345cfa2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3x}{4\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368354} {:answer/id #uuid "e756ccee-191e-5f8c-ad6f-5baa620bb61c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368355} {:answer/id #uuid "0e8af9a5-eef4-597d-8a2e-bb0dd59701c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{6x}{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368356}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368355} :answer-field/id #uuid "df91d777-147f-5107-937e-39046d30908d" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368352} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0d30efb-5e3f-5069-bdd1-edd81b1cb1ec" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368358} {:answer/id #uuid "3fe8b930-35c5-54c4-94ff-65189b49b3c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368359} {:answer/id #uuid "f4a07916-0005-53d4-80ac-6d722dff7217" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368360} {:answer/id #uuid "eb23d851-2d00-5cda-9fc1-a2a5b81ab7ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "n=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368361}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368359} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e418679-4b7e-51b6-a85a-3056974bca2f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368357}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "5d1812d8-39e4-5dd4-b731-c6eb976d76d3" :question/math-academy-id "q-343896" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x),$ where the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by \n\n$$\nf(x)∼2+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{1}{{n}^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options in the reasoning below regarding the resonant component of a particular solution of the equation.\n\nThe natural frequency of the system matches the frequency of the {{field-1}} harmonic of the forcing function. \n\nConsequently, the particular solution contains a resonant term of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}={{field-2}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We begin by identifying resonance. The differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=f(x)\n$$\n\nhas a natural frequency of \n\n$$\n\\omega =\\sqrt{\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nThe frequencies of the forcing harmonics for a $6$-periodic function $(L=3)$ are \n\n$$\n{\\omega }_{n}=\\frac{n\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFor $n=1$, we have ${\\omega }_{1}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ Since ${\\omega }_{1}=\\omega ,$ the $n=1$ harmonic of the forcing function causes resonance.\n\nFor the $n=1$ harmonic, the forcing term is \n\n$$\n{f}_{1}(x)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nBecause this frequency matches the natural frequency, we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{{p}_{1}}^{′} & =(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}))+x(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})) \\\\ {y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″} & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}x(A\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+B\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{{p}_{1}}^{″}$ and ${y}_{{p}_{1}}$ into the equation ${y}^{″}+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}):$\n\n$$\n[-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})-\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{1}}]+\\frac{{\\pi }^{2}}{9}{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})\n$$\n\nThe resonant terms involving $x$ cancel out, leaving\n\n$$\n-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}B\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3})=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the sine and cosine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}A=0 \\\\ \\frac{2\\pi }{3}B=1\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}A=0 \\\\ B=\\frac{3}{2\\pi }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the resonant component of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{{p}_{1}}=\\frac{3x}{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi x}{3}).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "8730a614-36f9-5c60-80e1-eac2fd3a8305" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512998}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513002 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368337 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2077a0e4-25ea-549f-b18b-eeaee168ff0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368339} {:answer/id #uuid "cf025f55-d758-53ad-ba9a-e6003ea863d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368340} {:answer/id #uuid "4926bd85-afe3-53d9-b42e-08dd5f26bb22" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368341} {:answer/id #uuid "70f39c4d-5178-5b56-a752-489a2b9d7ed4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368342}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368341} :answer-field/id #uuid "099f2aa9-cfdf-59eb-bd13-a3254f3a71f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368338} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb9c3ae4-a918-591f-9eba-4ebc030650cd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+54)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368344} {:answer/id #uuid "b0fe33c1-fafa-55ee-a956-b6576f020afa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+54)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368345} {:answer/id #uuid "079feac5-65d5-5c90-8e5d-94615aae162a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+54)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368346} {:answer/id #uuid "78348f45-f808-5940-ba48-bfbdb844fa02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+54)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368347}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186368345} :answer-field/id #uuid "1055c2d2-985c-506c-98b6-2c511358a08c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186368343}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3769a7a2-440c-52c1-8785-e3df2dc4c9a6" :question/math-academy-id "q-343885" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{″}-6y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼6+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\n*Hint: You may assume that all the cosine coefficients in the Fourier series of the particular solution are equal to zero.*\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{{field-1}}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-2}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\ {y}_{p}^{″} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{″}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation and have\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{b}_{n}{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})-6(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))=6+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n-6\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}-6)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})=6+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and sine coefficients, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-6\\cdot \\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=6 \\\\ {b}_{n}(-\\frac{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{9}-6)=\\frac{2(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}\\pi }\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}=-1 \\\\ {b}_{n}=\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+54)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼-1+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{18(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{3}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+54)}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9099693d-f185-5899-981c-a69e22a33e27" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513000}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513019 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512930 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4cb46238-0858-5e29-93f5-3b1abf6e8453" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512264} {:answer/id #uuid "5bc4c62d-f986-5b1e-8753-4ec4676a3b8f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{15(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512311} {:answer/id #uuid "b6d76ec8-e500-538c-8442-85b23edc88a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512535}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512535} :answer-field/id #uuid "4e6a7300-844d-5db4-8005-d85823b210da" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512770} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9336c13e-c8cb-5577-898b-73fc979337d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{45(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512448} {:answer/id #uuid "9e473653-b9e6-5e7c-a302-54a5b439449d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512469} {:answer/id #uuid "dadc4114-78f9-5557-a713-9afe7cf481ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512621}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186512469} :answer-field/id #uuid "a5322b59-ac05-57c5-bebd-3ff95532c124" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512813}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b98de4ab-a079-5d39-b165-ba7998caa236" :question/math-academy-id "q-343834" :question/problem "Select the missing parts in a particular solution of the differential equation ${y}^{′}+5y=f(x),$ where the Fourier series of the $6$-periodic forcing function is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nThe Fourier series of a particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,{{field-1}}\\cdot cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{{field-2}}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We assume that a solution of our equation has the following Fourier series:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the derivative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & ∼\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\cdot (-\\frac{n\\pi }{3}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))+{b}_{n}\\cdot \\frac{n\\pi }{3}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we substitute the expressions for ${y}_{p}^{′}$ and ${y}_{p}$ into the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & ({\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,+5(\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+{b}_{n}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})) \\\\  & \\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRearranging the terms, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & \\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(\\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n})cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }(-\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{b}_{n})sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}) \\\\  & \\,\\,\\,\\,={\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, equating the free terms and the cosine and sine coefficients, we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}\\frac{5{a}_{0}}{2}=0 \\\\ \\frac{{b}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n}=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi } \\\\ -\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{3}+5{b}_{n}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, ${a}_{0}=0.$ \n\nNext, from the third equation, \n\n$$\n{b}_{n}=\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation gives\n\n$$\n(\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15})\\frac{n\\pi }{3}+5{a}_{n}=\\frac{3(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi }\\,⇒\\,{a}_{n}=\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\n$$\n\nTherefore, for ${b}_{n}$ with $n\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{n} & =\\frac{{a}_{n}n\\pi }{15} \\\\  & =\\frac{n\\pi }{15}\\cdot \\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)} \\\\  & =\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our particular solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{135(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}\\pi ({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})+\\frac{9(-1)^{n}}{n({n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}+225)}\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "d60f6806-c6cb-503b-a2c8-fff99cda0786" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186513002}}] :activity/title "Solving ODEs Using Fourier Series" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186512085}]}
